The threat model, the view each party has and what a coalition of clients can
learn, is stated in \cref{sec:threat}.
%%
# 2026-08-23, W2. One word is new, "no". The original read "Neither holds a
# decryption key", where "neither" ranged over the aggregation server and the
# serving party. Those are one party now, and "neither" has no singular form
# that keeps "a". Everything else in the paragraph is the original with the
# clause naming the second party deleted.
\paragraph{View of the server} Ciphertexts under the collective public key, the
public per-client sample counts, and the evaluation keys. It holds no decryption
key, and by \cref{sec:mhe} it cannot decrypt. The server additionally sees
encrypted query features, which it cannot read and therefore cannot invert.
